drills.solution:
Split the cross into three one-gene crosses and find each probability (a 2 × 2 Punnett square for each, or the gamete probabilities: AA gives A with probability 1, Aa gives A with probability $\tfrac12$, aa never gives A).

- Gene A, {a1} × {a2}: P({ta}) = {pa:frac}
- Gene B, {b1} × {b2}: P({tb}) = {pb:frac}
- Gene C, {c1} × {c2}: P({tc}) = {pc:frac}

The genes are independent, so multiply (product rule): ${pa:texfrac} \times {pb:texfrac} \times {pc:texfrac} =$ **{p:frac}**.

A cross of two double heterozygotes gives 16 equally likely outcomes: 9 with dominant alleles at both genes, 3 and 3 with a dominant allele at only one gene, and 1 homozygous recessive at both. Epistasis merges some of these classes:

- mice: 9 agouti (A_C_) : 3 black (aaC_) : 4 albino (A_cc and aacc);
- sweet peas: 9 purple (C_P_) : 7 white (C_pp, ccP_ and ccpp);
- squash: 12 white (W_Y_ and W_yy) : 3 yellow (wwY_) : 1 green (wwyy);
- shepherd's purse: 15 triangular : 1 oval (aabb).

The class asked about makes up $\dfrac{{sc:int}}{16}$ of the offspring, so expect $\dfrac{{sc:int}}{16} \times {total} =$ **{cnt}**.

